WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:08.740
PRESENTER: The origin of
facial tissue may be Japanese.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.040
A 17th-Century
historical account

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.180
describes Japanese blowing their
noses in small soft papers then

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.920
throwing them on the ground.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.020
Modern day tissues
were first marketed

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.050
in the early 1920s
as a disposable cloth

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.490
for wiping off cold cream.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.560
While some sneezers still
prefer traditional cloth

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.200
handkerchiefs, the
disposable paper tissue

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.270
is the implement of
choice with the sniffling

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.290
and lens-cleaning masses.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.880
This brand is made entirely
of recycled paper--

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.760
specifically, used
computer printer paper--

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.595
because its short, flexible
fibers produce soft tissue.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.130
The half-ton bales go into a
giant machine called a pulper.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.500
It breaks up the
paper into fibers

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.150
and mixes them with water.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.010
The result is called pulp.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.660
Next, it injects
the pulp with air.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.410
This causes the ink to
detach from the fibers

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.379
and cling to the air bubbles
which rise to the top

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:06.640
and drain off.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.100
The machine then feeds
the now ink-free pulp

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.690
through several rollers.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.030
Like an old-fashioned
wringer washing machine,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.060
the rollers squeeze
out the dirty water.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.370
A screw conveyor then
breaks up the pulp

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.590
and moves it to the
next station which

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.690
rinses it with clean water.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.610
Now the pulp is ready
to become tissue.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.020
That transformation begins
in the paper machine.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.080
It injects the pulp evenly
across the screen conveyor

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.960
belt, then the pulp
passes through rollers

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.730
that press out the water.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.820
The extracted water drains
down through the screen.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.060
The pulp then passes through
a hot air dryer and exits

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.655
the machine as a thin
10-foot wide sheet of paper.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.300
Each jumble roll
coming off the machine

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.700
contains about 37
miles of paper.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.600
The converting machine
is the giant contraption

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.915
that now transforms
this paper into tissues.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.110
The first station unwinds
two rolls of paper,

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.979
applying modest tension to
remove waves and wrinkles.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.900
The next station
mates the two papers,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.370
producing a two-ply sheet.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.410
The following station holds
the sheet steady with suction,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.290
as a knife slices across
it every 8 and 1/2 inches.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.020
You can see the
cuts in slow motion.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.470
At the next station,
these two-ply sheets

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.090
meet up with two-ply
sheets coming

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.020
from the opposite direction.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.035
Here's what that looks like in
slow motion, and at full speed.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.900
The sheets enter a mechanism
that folds them in half,

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.970
in an interlocking fashion.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.310
In slow motion, you can
see how they interlock--

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.470
each sheet folded in half, one
side inserted into the fold

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.280
of the next sheet.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.980
This happens at a speed
of 16 folds per second.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.650
This produces a huge stack
of folded tissue 5 feet wide.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.300
The next station separates
the big stack into small ones,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.300
in preparation for
the final cutting.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.730
Each smaller stack
contains the precise number

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:27.860
of tissues the tissue box will
contain, from 80 to 250 sheets,

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.295
depending on the format
they're packaging.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.880
The smaller stacks now
travel to the next station

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.390
where an automated circular saw
cuts every 8 inches, producing

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.515
the final tissue size.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.110
The interlocking
folds ensure that when

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:00.240
you pull out one tissue, it
draws the next out of the box

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.040
ready to use.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.330
To produce three-ply tissues,
the converting machine

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.180
processes six rolls
into two sheets of three

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.480
plies each then folds them in
the same interlocking fashion.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.360
The finished tissues
travel by conveyor belt

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.070
to the automated packaging line.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.040
A robot with multiple
suction-cupped arms grabs

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.300
flattened boxes one at a
time, opens them, and lines

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.240
them up on another conveyor belt
running alongside the tissue

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.340
belt. An automated arm
compresses the tissues

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.700
and slides them into the box.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.900
The next station glues
the flaps closed.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.300
The top of the box has
a removable tab with

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.030
clear plastic film underneath.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.040
A tight slide in the
film makes grabbing

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.210
a single tissue a non-issue.

